{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778780887,"nonce":"7/UllCbsH0n5CQXfnHh7KAvyd9w=","title":"Expose Feedbax as a Mandible plugin/provider for experiment specs, components, runs, and artifacts","message":"## Problem\n\nFeedbax should be usable as a specialized experiment workbench inside a broader Mandible-managed research ecosystem. Mandible may own project memory, issue/work graphs, agent sessions, artifact storage, and review/auth flows; Feedbax should expose what it can do through stable contracts rather than requiring Mandible to hardcode Feedbax internals.\n\nThis requires Feedbax to define a provider/plugin boundary: schemas, capabilities, validation endpoints, component registries, import/export manifests, artifact publication, and UI entry points such as \"Open in Feedbax Studio\".\n\n## Desired Architecture\n\nFeedbax acts as a domain plugin/provider. It should be able to declare:\n\n- Which spec types it accepts: graph/project specs, model/task/loss specs, training run specs, evaluation run specs, analysis specs, report specs.\n- Which artifacts it can produce: checkpoints, histories, trajectory datasets, evaluation outputs, figures, tables, reports, manifests.\n- Which UI actions it supports: open project, inspect run set, launch training, run evaluation, run analysis, view report.\n- Which schemas and component registries are available to users and agents.\n- Which local extension points exist for adding new components, losses, tasks, analyses, or report transforms.\n\nMandible can then conditionally show Feedbax actions only when the Feedbax provider is installed and the selected issue/spec/artifact is compatible.\n\n## Contract Shape\n\nFeedbax should expose a machine-readable capability manifest. A first pass could be JSON Schema/OpenAPI-oriented:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"plugin\": \"feedbax\",\n  \"kind\": \"experiment_provider\",\n  \"version\": \"...\",\n  \"capabilities\": {\n    \"open_project\": { \"input_schema\": \"FeedbaxProjectRef\" },\n    \"validate_graph_spec\": { \"input_schema\": \"GraphSpec\" },\n    \"validate_training_spec\": { \"input_schema\": \"TrainingRunSpec\" },\n    \"start_training_run\": { \"input_schema\": \"TrainingRunSpec\", \"output_schema\": \"TrainingRunManifest\" },\n    \"start_evaluation_run\": { \"input_schema\": \"EvaluationRunSpec\", \"output_schema\": \"EvaluationRunManifest\" },\n    \"run_analysis\": { \"input_schema\": \"AnalysisRunSpec\", \"output_schema\": \"AnalysisRunManifest\" },\n    \"register_component\": { \"input_schema\": \"ComponentPackageSpec\" }\n  },\n  \"schemas\": {\n    \"GraphSpec\": \"...\",\n    \"ComponentSpec\": \"...\",\n    \"TrainingRunManifest\": \"...\",\n    \"EvaluationRunManifest\": \"...\",\n    \"AnalysisRunManifest\": \"...\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe specific transport is open: local HTTP endpoints from Studio, a CLI, MCP-like tools, or a small plugin manifest file plus commands. The important property is that agents and Mandible can discover capabilities and validate specs before execution.\n\n## Component and Extension Story\n\nAgents running under Mandible should be able to ask Feedbax what components exist and what a valid new component must look like. A local component-registration path should support:\n\n- Component metadata: name, category, ports, parameter schema, icon/display hints.\n- Runtime implementation: Python import path, builder function, serialization hooks, optional tests.\n- Compatibility declarations: which graph contexts it can appear in, input/output type constraints, supported training/eval backends.\n- Provenance: created by agent/session/issue, reviewed status, version/hash.\n\nThis lets an agent create a new Feedbax component when a project needs it, then submit it through normal review/provenance rather than mutating Studio state invisibly.\n\n## Artifact and Manifest Integration\n\nFeedbax should not assume it owns long-term storage for large artifacts. It should emit manifests that reference artifact IDs provided by the orchestrator/artifact store, for example:\n\n- `TrainingRunSetManifest`\n- `TrainingRunManifest`\n- `EvaluationRunManifest`\n- `AnalysisRunManifest`\n- `ReportManifest`\n\nLarge outputs such as `.eqx` checkpoints, trajectory arrays, histories, logs, and rendered figures can be handed to Mandible's artifact store and returned as stable content-addressed references. Feedbax manifests then refer to these stable IDs rather than fragile absolute paths.\n\n## Design Questions\n\n- Which parts of the contract belong in core `feedbax`, and which are Studio-only?\n- Should Feedbax expose provider capabilities through HTTP, CLI, Python entry points, static plugin manifests, or some combination?\n- How do project packages register additional components/analyses without making Mandible know project-specific imports?\n- How should Feedbax validate specs generated by agents before any training/evaluation starts?\n- How does Feedbax distinguish deterministic reports from agent-authored narrative reports?\n- What is the smallest MVP that supports \"Open in Feedbax Studio\" from a Mandible graph node and publish result manifests/artifacts back?\n\n## Related\n\n- `8d94424` — Feedbax design coordination.\n- `93f79f2` — Feedbax Studio design coordination.\n- `e33f487` — run manifests as canonical lineage with database indexing.\n- `4e0d925` — Studio bottom pane should operate on pipeline collections.\n- Mandible `0516fc0` — Mandible design coordination.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}